  "question": {
    "text": "A rigid body rotates about a fixed axis with variable angular velocity equal to $\\alpha-\\beta t$, at the time $t$, where $\\alpha, \\beta$ are constants. The angle through which it rotates before it stops is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Fg09K",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\alpha^2}{2 \\beta}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "h74tA",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\alpha^2-\\beta^2}{2 \\alpha}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Ln7Hx",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\alpha^2-\\beta^2}{2 \\beta}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "tIifZ",
      "text": "$\\frac{(\\alpha-\\beta) \\alpha}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\omega=\\alpha-\\beta t$. Comparing with $\\omega=\\omega_0-\\alpha t$\nInitial angular velocity $=\\alpha$\nAngular'retardation $=\\beta$\n$\\therefore$ Angle rotated before it stops is $\\frac{\\alpha^2}{2 \\beta}$. [using\n$\\left.\\omega^2=\\omega_0^2+2 \\beta \\theta\\right]$",
    "image": null
  },
